    "q-337177": {
      "dom_id": "step-q337177",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y={e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below. \n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}.",
      "worked_solution": "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{4x})}^{′}-4(u{e}^{4x}) & ={e}^{4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4u{e}^{4x}-4u{e}^{4x} & ={e}^{4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}} & ={e}^{4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x} & ={e}^{4x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{4x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
